From uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com  Tue Oct 25 16:15:22 2022
From: uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com (Finn Lindgren)
Date: Tue, 25 Oct 2022 16:15:22 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] problems with ipoints() and inla.spTransform() when using S^2 mesh in Cartesian xyz
Message-ID: <1f6Lj6ZbbEc.qdrqoxe_AgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <1f6Lj6ZbbEc.46w27bS-AgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <1f6Lj6ZbbEc.AtpuVhGxAgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com> <1f6Lj6ZbbEc.46w27bS-AgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/1f6Lj6ZbbEc/m/qdrqoxe_AgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The mesh transformation may _run_, but the resulting mesh isn't usable
for _correct_ computations, as it becomes a deformed flattened mesh
that folds in on itself.
The correct solution is to transform and calculate on the sphere
instead of in projections; no projection handles the full globe
properly.

I have a PhD student who's working on polygon-aggregated data
problems, and coe for this will be making its way into the package.

Finn

